var RG = window.RG;

/* Book study, part 3: Chapters 11-15 of a 30-chapter popular book on union with Christ,
   the 'mirror' reading of the gospel, inclusion and identity.
   Everything below is paraphrase in our own words; the book's author is not named.
   Scripture quotations, where used, are from the World English Bible (WEB).
   Where the book leans on word studies that most scholars would question, we say so. */

RG.deCh3 = [
  {
    "id": "ch11-economy-of-inclusion",
    "n": 11,
    "title": "The logic of God's economy of inclusion",
    "big": "In God's way of reckoning, Jesus stands for the whole human race, so what happened to him happened to all of us, and faith is waking up to that fact rather than making it true.",
    "summary": "The chapter argues that the whole story of Scripture turns on God's image and likeness, shown and rescued in Jesus. Its key is Paul's little phrase <em>in Christ</em>: God placed humanity in his Son before anyone believed anything, so our inclusion is neither a lucky draw nor the result of our own choice. Working verse by verse through Ephesians 1, Romans 5, 2 Corinthians 5 and 10, and Colossians 1 and 2, it builds a logic of representation: one man's disobedience showed humanity as sinners, and one man's obedience shows humanity as righteous. When Jesus died, all died, and all were raised with him, so no one may now be known by mere human labels. The cross cancelled the record of guilt, disarmed every system that ranks people by performance, and ends with every knee bowing in glad worship. The chapter closes on the claim that the only faith that finally matters is what God believes about us.",
    "points": [
      { "head": "Jesus represents the human race", "text": "The theme running through the whole Bible is God's image and likeness, shown and rescued in Jesus. In God's mind, Jesus is not one person among many but the one in whom all humanity is represented. Everything else in the chapter flows from this idea of representation." },
      { "head": "Four circles of union", "text": "John 14:20 says: <em>In that day you will know that I am in my Father, and you in me, and I in you.</em> The book pictures four circles nested inside one another: the Father, Jesus in the Father, us in Jesus, and the Spirit in us. The incarnation does not split the Trinity; it celebrates humanity's inclusion. Crucially, our knowing does not put us there. Knowing only wakes us up to a oneness that is already true." },
      { "head": "'In Christ' is the key code", "text": "The book calls Paul's phrase <em>in Christ</em> the PIN code of the Bible. Ephesians 1:3-4 says every blessing is given us in Christ and that we were chosen in him before the <em>foundation</em> of the world. The book reads the Greek <em>katabole</em> as 'falling away' and so renders it 'before the fall', meaning God found us in Christ before we were lost in Adam. Most translators and lexicons read it as 'foundation' or 'laying down', so this rendering is a minority view, but the point it serves (God's plan for us comes first) does not depend on it." },
      { "head": "Neither a lucky draw nor our vote", "text": "Our place in Christ, the book insists, is not the result of a secret selection of a few, which is its pointed objection to Calvinist election. Nor is it the result of our own decision to follow Jesus, since something does not become true by popular vote or by our believing it. Faith is what happens to us when we meet the good news. 1 Corinthians 1:30 is the anchor: it is God's doing that we are in Christ." },
      { "head": "Missing inclusion misses the cross", "text": "If evangelical teaching overlooks that humanity was included in Christ before it knew or believed, the book says it misses the point of his death, descent, resurrection and ascension. Salvation then shrinks into each person's private fate, managed by religious institutions, with Jesus kept as a sentimental figure from history. Instead, by dying our death once for all, Jesus walked through death into our deepest darkness and lostness and led us out as his trophies (Ephesians 4:7-10). Ephesians 2:5-6 says this happened while we were still dead: we were made alive, raised and seated with him." },
      { "head": "Adoption as coming of age", "text": "In Ephesians 1:5 the word usually translated adoption, <em>huiothesia</em>, is explained not as a Western legal adoption of an outsider but as a son's formal coming of age, something like a Jewish bar mitzvah. Galatians 4:6 adds that the Spirit of the Son cries 'Abba, Father' in our hearts. The picture is of children recognised as full heirs, not strangers taken in." },
      { "head": "Highly favoured in the Beloved", "text": "Ephesians 1:6 uses <em>charitoo</em>, to grace or highly favour, a word found elsewhere in the New Testament only in the angel's greeting to Mary (Luke 1:28). God's love for his Son is his love for us. So the book says the gospel is not about telling people how lost they are, but reminding them how loved they are." },
      { "head": "Everything summed up in Christ", "text": "Ephesians 1:10 speaks of God's <em>oikonomia</em>, his household management or administration, in which all things in heaven and earth are gathered up in Christ. The verb <em>anakephalaiomai</em> (up plus head) is read as a return to our source where no separation exists, and its aorist form as a completed whole. Ephesians 1:11 and Romans 8:29 add that Christ is the measure of our portion: we were designed to fit the mould of his sonship, and our original shape is preserved in him." },
      { "head": "One man's act and the many", "text": "Romans 5:18-19 sets the logic out plainly: one trespass led to condemnation for all, and one righteous act leads to justification of life for all. The verb <em>kathistemi</em> means to set up or exhibit, so the book stresses that we were not made sinners by our own disobedience, nor made righteous by our own obedience. Both were done to us by a representative." },
      { "head": "One died for all, therefore all died", "text": "2 Corinthians 5:14 in the WEB reads: <em>For the love of Christ constrains us; because we judge thus, that one died for all, therefore all died.</em> The book explains <em>sunecho</em> as to press together, hold and even resonate, and <em>ara</em> as 'therefore', a conclusion. Jesus did not die 99 per cent, or for 99 per cent. If Paul had limited the second half of the verse to those who qualify, he would have had to limit the first half too. Verses 15-16 follow: all were included in his resurrection, so from now on we know no one by merely human labels, not even Christ himself." },
      { "head": "The obedience of Christ, not our obedience", "text": "2 Corinthians 10:4-5 speaks of tearing down strongholds and taking every thought captive. The verb <em>aichmalotizo</em> pictures arresting a thought at spear point. Most versions say 'to obey Christ'; the book reads it as 'the obedience of Christ', the obedience he already completed (the Greek genitive can carry either sense). The difference matters: obeying Christ by our own effort keeps us under the same shame and failure as the law, while his finished obedience has already secured every blessing and dwarfs Adam's failure (Romans 5:12-21). The book calls this understanding the mind's best anti-virus protection." },
      { "head": "Darkness is only the absence of light", "text": "Light dispels darkness effortlessly. Darkness does not erase what is true; it only hides it. John 1:9 says the true light that enlightens everyone was coming into the world, dividing history into before and after Christ. Colossians 1:13 says we were rescued from the authority of darkness, which the book calls the sense-ruled world of performance; what gave darkness its power was the lie we believed about ourselves. Colossians 1:15 adds that in Jesus the invisible God became visible, so God can never be invisible again." },
      { "head": "Sin as losing your form", "text": "On Colossians 1:14 the book explains <em>hamartia</em>, sin, as 'without form' or without one's allotted portion (from <em>a</em>, not, and <em>meros</em>, part), the opposite of <em>metamorphoo</em>, being transformed into form. Sin is living out of tune with our design. It links this to Deuteronomy 32:18, where the Hebrew verb for God giving us birth is said to also mean dancing, so Israel forgot the God who danced with them. Both of these are the book's own readings: standard lexicons explain <em>hamartia</em> as 'missing the mark', and the Hebrew verb (<em>chul</em>) usually means to writhe in birth, though it is used of dancing in Judges 21:21." },
      { "head": "The record of guilt wiped clean", "text": "Colossians 2:14 says Christ blotted out the handwritten record against us and nailed it to the cross. <em>Exaleipho</em>, to wipe out, is linked to removing a grease stain, so guilt and hurtful memories were like stains on the conscience, now washed away (compare Acts 3:19). <em>Cheirographon</em> is a document in one's own handwriting, and <em>dogma</em> is linked to thought patterns, so humanity had in effect signed its own death sentence through constant failure under the law. When people nailed Jesus to the tree in religious fury, they did not see that he was becoming the scapegoat for the whole race (Isaiah 53:4-5)." },
      { "head": "Every ranking system disarmed", "text": "Colossians 2:15 says he stripped the rulers and authorities and made a public show of them. The book reads <em>apekduomai</em> as stripping off a disguise, so the religious mask that made the law of works the measure of a person was openly defeated, and guilt lost its power to blackmail. Drawing on 1 Corinthians 15:24, it argues that every <em>arche</em> (rank), <em>exousia</em> (authority based on 'what I can do better than you') and <em>dunamis</em> (willpower) will be brought to nothing (<em>katargeo</em>) and placed under the rule of grace. 1 Corinthians 2:7-8 adds that had the rulers grasped this mystery, they would never have crucified the Lord of glory." },
      { "head": "From the depths to every knee bowing", "text": "Philippians 2:8-11 frames the cross: fully God, Jesus became fully man to the point of dying a criminal's death at the hands of his own creation, and through death descended into our darkness; he now holds the keys of death and Hades (Revelation 1:18). God then exalted him and gave him the name above every name, and every knee will bow and every tongue confess. The book reads this as spontaneous worship, rooted in Isaiah 45:22-23, where God says to all the ends of the earth 'be saved', and swears by himself (the Hebrew <em>shaba</em>, linked to the number seven, an oath that ends all dispute). Philippians 2:12-13 then calls us to work out this salvation, with God himself as our inner source of desire and energy. The only faith that ultimately matters, it concludes, is not what we believe about God but what God believes about us." }
    ],
    "illustrations": [
      { "title": "Four nested circles", "text": "Picture four circles, one inside another: the Father, then Jesus in the Father, then us in Jesus, then the Spirit in us. The image is meant to show inseparable, intimate oneness. Our knowing does not draw the circles; it only lets us see them." },
      { "title": "Gold that is discovered", "text": "Gold does not become gold when someone finds it, but once it is found it becomes currency. So our union with Christ is true before we know it, and knowing it is what makes it spendable in daily life." },
      { "title": "Grease stains on the conscience", "text": "The word for blotting out is linked to wiping away grease. Guilt and painful memories cling to the conscience like stains on cloth, and the cross is pictured as cleaning them out completely." },
      { "title": "A mind's anti-virus", "text": "The book compares understanding Christ's finished obedience to anti-virus software for the mind. Any thought that threatens our redeemed identity is caught and taken captive before it can do damage." },
      { "title": "The absent apostle", "text": "In Philippians 2:12 Paul says to work out your salvation even more in his absence. The book takes this as the mark of good ministry: Paul did not bind people to himself but to his message, so his absence made no difference to their faith." }
    ],
    "words": [
      { "word": "katabole", "meaning": "Usually translated 'foundation' (of the world). The book reads it as 'falling away', giving 'before the fall of the world'. This is a minority reading." },
      { "word": "katenopion", "meaning": "Before, in the presence of. The book stresses the closest possible nearness: face to face." },
      { "word": "huiothesia", "meaning": "Adoption, literally 'placing as a son'. Explained here as a coming of age into full sonship rather than taking in an outsider." },
      { "word": "charitoo", "meaning": "To grace, to favour highly. Used in Ephesians 1:6 and in the greeting to Mary in Luke 1:28." },
      { "word": "oikonomia", "meaning": "Household management, administration, economy. God's way of arranging his household, which gives the chapter its title." },
      { "word": "anakephalaiomai", "meaning": "To sum up or gather under one head (up plus head). Read here as all things returning to their source in Christ." },
      { "word": "kathistemi", "meaning": "To set up, appoint or exhibit. Used in Romans 5:19 of the many being 'made' sinners or righteous by one person's act." },
      { "word": "sunecho", "meaning": "To hold together, press, constrain. The book adds the sense of holding close and resonating, as Christ's love grips Paul." },
      { "word": "aichmalotizo", "meaning": "To take captive, from 'spear' and 'capture'. Pictured as arresting every hostile thought at spear point." },
      { "word": "hamartia", "meaning": "Sin. The book derives it from 'without form or portion', meaning living out of tune with our design; the standard sense is 'missing the mark'." },
      { "word": "exaleipho", "meaning": "To wipe out or blot out, as writing is erased. Linked here to wiping away a grease stain." },
      { "word": "cheirographon", "meaning": "A handwritten document, such as a certificate of debt. The record of guilt nailed to the cross." },
      { "word": "apekduomai", "meaning": "To strip off, disarm. Christ stripped the powers, which the book reads as unmasking the religion of performance." },
      { "word": "katargeo", "meaning": "To render inactive or bring to nothing. Read as freedom from all self-effort to improve what God has already completed." }
    ],
    "questions": [
      { "q": "If everyone is already in Christ, why does faith matter?", "a": "The book's answer is that faith does not create the reality; it awakens us to it, like discovered gold becoming usable currency. Unbelief does not change God's verdict, but it does keep a person living as if it were not true. Many traditional readers would answer differently, holding that union with Christ is applied personally through faith and the Spirit, not simply discovered." },
      { "q": "Does 2 Corinthians 5:14 really mean all humanity died with Christ?", "a": "The book argues that the logic of the verse forbids limiting the second half without limiting the first. Many Reformed interpreters read 'all' as all for whom Christ died, meaning the elect, while others read it as all people but stress that the benefits are received by faith. It is a genuinely debated text, and the book takes the most inclusive reading." },
      { "q": "Is it fair to call our inclusion neither God's selection nor our choice?", "a": "The book rejects both a limited election and a decision-based salvation, placing everything in God's prior act in Christ. Calvinists would reply that election is in Christ and is pure grace, and Arminians would say God's grace invites a real human response. The book's point is that salvation starts with God's verdict, not our performance." },
      { "q": "What is the difference between 'obeying Christ' and 'the obedience of Christ'?", "a": "Obeying Christ, read as our effort, can keep us in the old cycle of trying, failing and feeling ashamed. The obedience of Christ is the finished work he did on our behalf, which the book says outweighs Adam's failure. Most translations read 2 Corinthians 10:5 the first way; the book argues for the second." }
    ],
    "scriptures": ["John 14:20", "Ephesians 1:3-11", "1 Corinthians 1:30", "Ephesians 2:5-6", "Romans 5:12-19", "2 Corinthians 5:14-16", "2 Corinthians 10:4-5", "John 1:9", "Colossians 1:13-15", "Colossians 2:14-15", "1 Corinthians 2:7-8", "1 Corinthians 15:24", "Philippians 2:8-13", "Isaiah 45:22-23", "Revelation 1:18"],
    "reflect": "If God's opinion of you was settled in Christ before you ever believed, what changes in how you see the people around you today?"
  },

  {
    "id": "ch12-identity-redeemed",
    "n": 12,
    "title": "Identity redeemed",
    "big": "The fall cost us the memory of who we are, not our worth to God, and Jesus came to show us our true face again and introduce us to ourselves.",
    "summary": "This chapter says the worst damage of the fall was that humanity lost its conviction of its true identity and slipped from 'I am' into 'I am not', living on a treadmill of performance. Jesus' parables of the lost sheep, coin and son show that neither ownership nor value was ever lost: you can only be lost if you belong. In Jesus the fullness of God lives in a human body, proving human life was made for God, and he is the mirror that shows us our original face, not an example to copy but a picture of us. Paul, Peter and James each discovered an identity deeper than their natural birth, and the book reads Peter's new name and the word for church as a call back to our true name. It closes by contrasting the Lamb's Book of Life, where our real identity is written, with the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, which it calls the 'I-am-not tree' of performance.",
    "points": [
      { "head": "The fall as a lost identity", "text": "The most tragic result of the fall, the book says, was losing the conviction of who we really are. A new rule took over, the treadmill of performance as the measure of worth, in a world ruled by chance and fading values. When Adam 'died', his awareness of God's likeness in him shrank to a guilt-based, performance-driven view of himself, his wife and God. He lost the sense of God's smile and began living a life that fades like seasonal flowers." },
      { "head": "From 'I am' to 'I am not'", "text": "The greatest contradiction of our true identity is living under an inferior opinion of ourselves, and we have inherited prejudices that cripple relationships at every level of society. The book sums the fall up as falling from 'I am' into 'I am not'. We have, in the words of James 1:24, forgotten what kind of people we are." },
      { "head": "Lost things still belong", "text": "In Luke 15, the lost sheep, lost coin and lost son were never stripped of their owner or their value. You cannot be lost unless you belong. The coin did not lose its stamped image when it rolled under the furniture, and the son 'came to himself' (Luke 15:17). So James 3:9 asks how we can bless God and curse people made in his likeness." },
      { "head": "Remembering who we are", "text": "Psalm 22, a prophetic picture of the crucifixion, ends with the promise that all the ends of the earth will remember and turn to the Lord. The book links this to Psalm 23, rendering the 'still waters' as waters of reflection where the soul remembers who it is, which is its own devotional reading (the Hebrew means waters of rest). Deuteronomy 32:18 and Isaiah 51:1 say we forgot the Rock we were cut from, and Ephesians 2:10 calls us God's workmanship, his <em>poiema</em>, his poetry." },
      { "head": "Human life is tailor-made for God", "text": "Colossians 1:15-19 and 2:9-10 say all the fullness of deity dwells bodily in Christ, and we are made complete in him. The book notes that <em>theotes</em>, deity, is a feminine noun, and that <em>katoikeo</em> means to make one's home. The universe cannot contain God, yet God is fully at home in a human body, which proves that human life was designed for God. Any teaching that leaves people with a sense of lack rather than completeness, it says, is a distraction from the truth." },
      { "head": "Jesus is God's final word", "text": "Hebrews 1:1-3 says God spoke in fragments through the prophets but has now spoken in a Son. The book takes <em>eschatos</em>, last, to mean God's final conclusion: what God said about humanity in Jesus is what 'last things' are really about. Jesus is God's language, the radiance of his glory and the exact imprint (<em>charakter</em>, an engraving) of his being. Having made purification for sins he sat down, so the book says his throne itself is proof of our redeemed innocence." },
      { "head": "Two characters, hand and forehead", "text": "Because <em>charakter</em> is related to the word used for the 'mark' of the beast in Revelation, the book suggests that either the Father's character or the character of the fallen mind will shape our actions (the hand) by first shaping our thoughts (the forehead). Isaiah 55:8-11 then promises that God's word, like rain and snow, will come down and soak the earth and not return empty. This is a symbolic reading; interpreters of Revelation differ widely on the mark." },
      { "head": "A mirror, not a model", "text": "Every human life is equally valued and represented in Christ, who shows us ourselves as in a mirror: not an example for us, but an example of us. The 'perfect law of liberty' in James 1:25 frees us to find our true self. The book says the mirror revelation turns the Bible into a new book: like a mirror, the page exists to show the face it reflects, and the most accurate translation of Scripture is the incarnation itself." },
      { "head": "Christ revealed in Paul", "text": "In Galatians 1:15-16 Paul says God was pleased to reveal his Son <em>in</em> him so that he might preach him among the nations. The book stresses the Greek <em>en emoi</em>, in me, and argues the second phrase means Christ revealed <em>in</em> the nations, not merely among them (most translations say 'among'). It notes that when Barnabas saw the Greeks at Antioch respond, he went to find Paul, and there the believers were first called Christians (Acts 11:20-26)." },
      { "head": "Offspring of God and a hidden treasure", "text": "In Acts 17:28 Paul tells Athenian thinkers that we live and move and have our being in God and are his offspring, quoting their own poets (Epimenides and Aratus). Jesus said to call no one on earth father, for we have one Father (Matthew 23:9). The book compares the seed of sonship in every person to seed lying in desert soil, waiting for rain (Isaiah 55:10-13), and to treasure hidden in a field (Matthew 13:44) and in clay jars (2 Corinthians 4:7)." },
      { "head": "Paul, Peter and James beyond natural birth", "text": "After his conversion Paul went to Arabia rather than Jerusalem, wanting to discover Christ in himself more than in history (Galatians 1:17), and later spent two weeks with Peter. The book notes Paul calls him Kephas, Aramaic for 'rock', to stress the name's meaning. Peter was told that flesh and blood had not revealed who Jesus was, and was then given a new name that revealed his own origin: a chip off the old block, hewn from the rock (Matthew 16:17-18). James, who did not believe during Jesus' ministry (John 7:5), saw the risen Jesus (1 Corinthians 15:7) and later wrote of seeing the face of our birth in the mirror of the word (James 1:17-24)." },
      { "head": "Church as a call back to our name", "text": "The book reads <em>ekklesia</em>, church, as from <em>ek</em> (out of, origin) and <em>kaleo</em> (to call or surname), so the church is built on the revelation of humanity's original identity. It reads Hades as 'not seeing', the walled city of the senses that cannot stand against the voice that calls us by name. The usual sense of <em>ekklesia</em> is an assembly of those called out; the 'surname' sense is the book's emphasis." },
      { "head": "We were not an accident", "text": "In Christ we discover we are not here by chance or simply by a parent's desire, but because God wanted to reveal himself in flesh. James 1:18 says he brought us forth by the word of truth, and 1 Corinthians 13:12 promises we will know as we have been known. John 1:14 says the Word made his dwelling among us and we beheld (<em>theaomai</em>, gazed upon) his glory, full of grace and truth." },
      { "head": "Grasshoppers and a defeated devil", "text": "Israel died in the desert not because their rescue from Egypt was inadequate but because they believed a lie about themselves: 'we were like grasshoppers' (Numbers 13:33), while the giants were already terrified of them (Joshua 2:11). The book says the church has preached a defeated devil back into business. The devil is not anyone's parent; he is the father of lies, and Malachi 2:10 asks: have we not all one Father?" },
      { "head": "Sin is missing out on sonship", "text": "Sin is singular and its symptoms are plural. The book says sin is not mainly about things you do or don't do, but about missing out on sonship. Even the resentful older brother in Luke 15 hears the father say: you are always with me, and all that is mine is yours. 1 John 1:1-4 then says the eternal life that was with (<em>pros</em>, face to face with) the Father has appeared, and we are invited into the fellowship of the Father and Son rather than inventing fellowship ourselves." },
      { "head": "Born from above, given back our name", "text": "Jesus told Nicodemus we must be born <em>anothen</em>, from above (John 3:3), and the book reads this as 'unless you originate from above', adding that Jesus quoted Psalm 82:6, 'you are gods' (John 10:34). In John 1:12-13 it reads the 'right to become' children as God confirming what already belongs to us: <em>didomi</em>, give, in the sense of giving back; <em>genesthai</em> in the aorist as a completed act; birth not from blood lines or whether we were a wanted child, but from God. 1 Peter 2:10-11 says we who were no people are now God's people. The chapter ends with the Lamb's Book of Life: having your name written there means finding your identity in the life the Lamb redeemed, and every false identity is burned away like dross from gold." }
    ],
    "illustrations": [
      { "title": "The ugly duckling", "text": "The duckling saw its reflection in the water and realised it was a swan. It did not need a facelift or lessons in acting like a swan. It only needed to know the truth about itself to be free." },
      { "title": "The coin under the furniture", "text": "A lost coin still carries its stamped image and inscription. Being lost changed its location, not its value or who owned it. So with people: you cannot be lost unless you belong." },
      { "title": "Seed in the desert", "text": "Seed lies hidden in soil everywhere, even in the desert, waiting for rain to wake it. The book uses this for the seed of sonship in every person, waiting for the word to fall like rain (Isaiah 55:10)." },
      { "title": "Mr Rock, a chip off the old block", "text": "Jesus renamed Simon son of Jonah 'Peter', the rock. The book hears this as Jesus saying: now that you know who I am, let me introduce you to who you are, hewn from the same Rock." },
      { "title": "Grasshoppers and giants", "text": "The spies said they felt like grasshoppers next to giants, yet Rahab later revealed the people of the land had melted with fear. Israel was defeated by their own self-image, not by their enemies." },
      { "title": "John at ninety", "text": "The book pictures the apostle John writing his first letter as an old man, still enjoying unhindered friendship with God and wanting to share it. Its playful aside: you are never too old to learn something new." }
    ],
    "words": [
      { "word": "poiema", "meaning": "Something made, workmanship (Ephesians 2:10). The book enjoys the link to the English word 'poem': we are God's poetry." },
      { "word": "theotes", "meaning": "Deity, godhood (Colossians 2:9). The book notes it is grammatically feminine." },
      { "word": "katoikeo", "meaning": "To dwell in, to settle and make one's home. God is fully at home in the human Jesus." },
      { "word": "eschatos", "meaning": "Last, final, uttermost. The book uses it to say that what God spoke in Jesus is his final conclusion about humanity." },
      { "word": "charakter", "meaning": "An engraving, stamp or exact impression (Hebrews 1:3). Jesus is the exact imprint of God's being." },
      { "word": "doxa", "meaning": "Glory. The book often traces it to <em>dokeo</em>, to think or seem, and so treats glory as God's opinion or intent about us." },
      { "word": "ekklesia", "meaning": "Assembly, church; usually explained as those called out. The book stresses <em>kaleo</em> as naming, so the church is a people called by their true name." },
      { "word": "anothen", "meaning": "From above, or again (John 3:3). The book reads it as originating from above." },
      { "word": "lambano", "meaning": "To take hold of, receive (John 1:12). Receiving Christ is grasping what is already ours." },
      { "word": "theaomai", "meaning": "To gaze at, behold with attention (John 1:14). We gazed in wonder at his glory." }
    ],
    "questions": [
      { "q": "If our value was never lost, what exactly did the fall damage?", "a": "The book says the fall damaged our awareness, not God's view of us: we began seeing ourselves through guilt and performance. Like the prodigal son, we wandered and forgot who we are, but we still belong. Traditional theology usually adds that the fall also brought real guilt and corruption needing atonement, which the book addresses in later chapters." },
      { "q": "Is Jesus an example for us to follow or not?", "a": "The book's slogan is that Jesus is not an example for us but of us, a mirror of our true self rather than a standard to strive toward. The New Testament does also call believers to imitate Christ (1 Peter 2:21), so many would say he is both: the picture of who we are and the pattern we grow into." },
      { "q": "Does calling everyone God's offspring mean everyone is already a child of God?", "a": "Acts 17:28 does call all people God's offspring by creation, and the book builds on this. Many traditional readers distinguish this creation sense from the adoptive sonship received through faith (John 1:12; Galatians 3:26). The book reads John 1:12 as God confirming an existing sonship rather than granting a new one." },
      { "q": "What does it mean to have your name in the Book of Life?", "a": "For the book, it means discovering your real identity in the life the Lamb has redeemed, rather than in the 'I-am-not' system of performance. It reads the lake of fire as a refining fire that burns away false identities like dross. Many interpreters read the Book of Life as a register of the saved and the lake of fire as final judgment, so this is a contested reading." }
    ],
    "scriptures": ["Luke 15", "Psalm 22:27", "Deuteronomy 32:18", "Isaiah 51:1", "Ephesians 2:10", "Colossians 1:15-19", "Colossians 2:9-10", "Hebrews 1:1-3", "Isaiah 55:8-13", "James 1:17-25", "Galatians 1:15-19", "Acts 17:28", "Matthew 16:13-18", "Numbers 13:33", "John 1:12-14", "John 3:3", "1 John 1:1-4", "1 Peter 2:10-11"],
    "reflect": "What 'I am not' statement about yourself would you need to put down if you believed God's opinion of you was the true one?"
  },

  {
    "id": "ch13-asia-in-one-man",
    "n": 13,
    "title": "God's strategy: Asia in one man",
    "big": "God always sees the many in the one, so the whole human race is represented in Jesus, and the good news can travel to whole cities and continents through a single person who sees it.",
    "summary": "The longest chapter in this section opens with Paul greeting Epaenetus, his first convert in Asia, as if one man stood for the whole province, and draws the principle that God always sees the many in the one. The law shows what happened to humanity in Adam; grace shows what happened to the same humanity in Christ, who died and rose as all of us. From there it ranges across Scripture: the Lamb who lifts the world's sin like an anchor, the resurrection as the receipt of our acquittal, Abraham as the father of nations, the righteousness of God revealed by his own faith, the Jubilee announced in Nazareth, and the angels' news of joy for all people. A large section looks at Paul's ministry: his weakness, sufferings, refusal of payment, servant titles and constant confidence that everyone is included, so that he could be all things to all people. It ends with stories of single people through whom whole regions heard, a harvest that is already ripe, and a call to be the light and the river rather than debate the darkness.",
    "points": [
      { "head": "The many in the one", "text": "Paul greets Epaenetus as the first convert of Asia (Romans 16:5), and the book hears him saying this one man represents the whole province to him. God always sees the many in the one: one died for all, therefore all died (2 Corinthians 5:14). The law reveals what happened to humanity in Adam, and grace reveals what happened to that same humanity in Christ. So the book's refrain: we were found in Christ before we were lost in Adam." },
      { "head": "A plan drawn before time", "text": "1 Corinthians 2:7-8 speaks of God's hidden wisdom, ordained before the ages for our glory. The book explains <em>proorizo</em> as marking out a horizon in advance, like an architect's detailed plan, and Romans 8:30 says those he marked out he also called, justified and glorified. Had the rulers understood this, they would not have crucified the Lord of glory. History recorded the death and resurrection of one man; eternity recorded the death and resurrection of the human race." },
      { "head": "The Lamb lifts the anchor", "text": "John the Baptist said: <em>Behold, the Lamb of God, who takes away the sin of the world!</em> (John 1:29). The verb <em>airo</em> means to lift up or take away, and the book pictures raising an anchor from the sea floor so the ship can sail free. This is not an angry god demanding a sacrifice but the Creator providing himself, going to absurd lengths to convince us of his love, which leaves religion nothing to bargain with. The book notes that 'sin' here is singular and <em>kosmos</em> means the whole human family, and links it to Jesus saying that when lifted up he would draw all to himself (John 12:32)." },
      { "head": "The resurrection is our receipt", "text": "Romans 4:25 in the WEB says Jesus <em>was delivered up for our trespasses, and was raised for our justification.</em> The book reads both uses of <em>dia</em> as 'because of': he was handed over because of our failures and raised because we were declared righteous, so the resurrection is the official receipt of our acquittal. If humanity were still guilty after he died, it says, the resurrection would be pointless. The word for trespass, <em>paraptoma</em>, is pictured as losing altitude, a bird that has stopped flying. Acts 17:31 adds that God has given proof to all by raising him from the dead. Most commentators read the second 'because of' as 'for the sake of' our justification, so this sequence is debated." },
      { "head": "The prophets saw you coming", "text": "1 Peter 1:10-12 says the prophets searched carefully about this salvation and the coming grace, with the Spirit of Christ in them pointing to his sufferings and the glory that would follow. The book stresses that salvation was always a grace revelation, never a reward for willpower, and that the prophets were serving us: their view reached beyond their own generation to you. Even angels long to look into it." },
      { "head": "No more 'us and them'", "text": "In Acts 17 Paul tells idol-worshipping philosophers that God is not far from any of us. The book says the incarnation cancels every definition of distance, so God is Immanuel to everyone and there can be no 'us and them'. God taught Peter the same lesson: call no person common or unclean (Acts 10:28). Being associated with Christ means you cannot think of one without the other, like two names permanently linked (Ephesians 1:3-4; 2 Corinthians 5:16)." },
      { "head": "Metanoia: a change of mind, not penance", "text": "The book says <em>metanoia</em>, usually rendered 'repentance', means a radical shift of mind (from 'with' and 'perceive'), a waking up to what is true, a gathering of one's thoughts. Faith is not a decision but a discovery. It blames the Latin <em>paenitentia</em> (penance) for the guilt-laden idea of repentance and claims the change was deliberate, to profit from people. In fairness: the Latin Vulgate's 'do penance' rendering was challenged by Erasmus and Luther, and the change-of-mind sense of <em>metanoia</em> is widely accepted, but the claim that a 're' was added to make money is not supported history, and in the New Testament <em>metanoia</em> does include turning from sin." },
      { "head": "Christ in you, in the nations", "text": "Colossians 1:25-29 calls Paul a steward of God's household whose job is to make known the mystery hidden for ages: Christ in you, the hope of glory. The book reads this as Christ indwelling every nation, the 'desire of nations' of Haggai 2:7, and Christ is not hiding in history, outer space, the future or the pages of Scripture, but is mirrored there to be unveiled in you. Paul's aim is to awaken every mind (<em>noutheteo</em>, explained as setting the mind right) and present everyone perfect in Christ, which the book calls an immediate reference rather than a distant goal." },
      { "head": "Abraham, father of nations", "text": "Galatians 3:8 says Scripture preached the gospel in advance to Abraham: in you all nations will be blessed. Romans 4:16-18 says the promise rests on grace so that it is secure for all his children, and that Abraham faced the God who calls into being things that do not exist. The book adds a run of observations: Terah fathered Abram unusually late, as if he were 'fathered from above'; God added the Hebrew letter <em>he</em> from his own name to Abram's; that letter is the number five, which it links to grace; the stars promised to Abraham are pictured falling like rain to become grains of sand; and <em>hamon</em> in 'father of a multitude' suggests the roar of a great crowd. Galatians 3:16 stresses one seed, Isaac the child of promise rather than Ishmael the child of effort, mirroring the Messiah. These letter and number readings are the book's own and speculative." },
      { "head": "God's faith and God's zeal", "text": "Romans 1:16-17 says the gospel reveals God's righteousness 'from faith to faith', and the book reads this as starting from God's own faith: it is about what God did right, not what Adam did wrong. It notes that Dike, the Greek goddess of justice, holds a pair of scales, while God's righteousness is not a balancing of our deeds. Isaiah 9:2-7 ends with 'the zeal of the Lord will do this', and the book contrasts God's zeal, which ignites, with religious zeal for God, which exhausts. Romans 10:2-4 says Israel had zeal without knowledge, trying to establish their own righteousness, when Christ is the end of the law for righteousness." },
      { "head": "The word is near you", "text": "Romans 10:6-10 says faith does not ask who will go up to heaven or down to the abyss to fetch Christ, because the word is near, in your mouth and heart (Deuteronomy 30:11-14). The book says he has already descended, defeated what held us captive and ascended with captives in his train (Ephesians 4:8-9). It points to the Transfiguration, where Moses and Elijah, the law and the prophets, appear and the Father says 'listen to him' (Luke 9:27-36; 2 Peter 1:16-21). Confessing (<em>homologeo</em>, saying the same thing) is our words echoing God's, and believing is something that happens to us when we see what God has done, not something we manufacture." },
      { "head": "Co-raised on the third day", "text": "1 Peter 1:3 says God gave us new birth through Jesus' resurrection, and the book links <em>anagennao</em>, born again, with being born from above. Hosea 6:2 in the WEB says: <em>After two days he will revive us. On the third day he will raise us up, and we will live before him.</em> The book calls this the only Old Testament text that names a third-day raising, and notes it says 'us'. Ephesians 2:5-6 uses completed tenses: we were made alive, raised and seated with Christ, and the perfect participle 'you have been saved' describes a finished act with ongoing effect. So we wasted time trying to get 'there' when there is where we already are (Colossians 3:1-3)." },
      { "head": "Today this is fulfilled", "text": "In Nazareth Jesus read Isaiah 61 (Luke 4:18-21): good news to the poor, release to captives, sight for the blind, freedom for the oppressed, the year of the Lord's favour. The book explains anointing (<em>chrio</em>, linked to the Hebrew <em>mashach</em>) as a hand drawn over, a measuring, so the anointing is the true measure of our lives; <em>aphesis</em>, release or forgiveness, is the same word the Greek Old Testament uses for the Jubilee (Leviticus 25:10), when everyone returned home. Its tense notes stress that the commissioning is complete and still in force. Then Jesus said: today this scripture is fulfilled in your hearing. The waiting game is over; it all now hangs on hearing." },
      { "head": "Good news of great joy for all people", "text": "The angel told the shepherds of great joy for all the people (Luke 2:10-11), and the book links Saviour, Christ and the related word <em>chrestos</em>, kind. On Luke 2:14 it notes that the oldest manuscripts read 'goodwill' in the genitive: not peace only to those who please God, but God's delight in humanity. He loved us at our worst and loved us first. The book is right that this reading is well supported, though translators still debate whether it means 'people of his goodwill' (those God favours) or God's goodwill toward humanity in general." },
      { "head": "Paul's secret: weakness and a full heart", "text": "Revelation like this sets the heart on fire: we cannot help speaking (Acts 4:20; Job 32:18-20; Psalm 39:3; Luke 24:32). Paul admitted his speech was considered weak (2 Corinthians 10:10; 11:6), came to Corinth trembling (1 Corinthians 2:1-5), and resolved to know nothing among them but Christ crucified. The book notes a manuscript variant in 2:1, 'mystery' versus 'testimony' of God, and reads 2:4 as pointing people away from the preacher to the Spirit's work in them. In 2 Corinthians 4 he renounces craftiness, commends truth to every conscience (literally 'joint seeing'), carries treasure in clay jars, is pressed but not crushed, and believes and therefore speaks (Psalm 116:10), and his catalogue of beatings, shipwrecks and hunger (2 Corinthians 11:23-30) shows that contentment (Philippians 4:11-13) and God's love (Romans 8:37-39) do not depend on circumstances." },
      { "head": "A ministry that is not for sale", "text": "Paul felt in debt to everyone, Greek and barbarian, educated and not (Romans 1:14), worked as a tentmaker without taking that as a title, and called apostles 'under-rowers', the lowest oarsmen on a galley (1 Corinthians 4:1). He shared his own life along with the gospel, like a nursing mother (1 Thessalonians 2:7-9), refused payment so no one could suspect his motives (1 Corinthians 9:15-18), and became all things to all people because he was persuaded of everyone's inclusion (9:19-23). Titus 1:7-9 and 3:2-6 add leaders who are hospitable, gentle and never quarrelsome, and salvation as God's mercy poured out like an artesian well, while one person can carry the news to a whole region (see the stories below), because the harvest is already ripe (John 4:35-38). The chapter ends: rather than debating degrees of darkness, be the light and the river of living water, because you are." }
    ],
    "illustrations": [
      { "title": "Epaenetus, all of Asia", "text": "Paul greets his first convert in Asia as if that one man stood for the whole province. The book makes this the key to God's strategy: he always sees the many in the one, and Jesus is the one in whom all are seen." },
      { "title": "Raising the anchor", "text": "The verb used for the Lamb 'taking away' sin can picture lifting an anchor off the sea floor. Once it is raised, the ship is free to sail. The book uses this for humanity freed from the weight of sin." },
      { "title": "The receipt of acquittal", "text": "The resurrection is pictured as the official receipt that our debt has been paid. A receipt does not pay the bill; it proves the bill was settled." },
      { "title": "Stars becoming sand", "text": "God told Abraham to count the stars and later promised descendants like the sand. The book imagines the stars falling like a drizzle of rain, each drop becoming a grain of sand, to show the sheer number of people his faith would touch." },
      { "title": "The Jubilee homecoming", "text": "In the Jubilee year every slave went free and every family returned to its land. The book links this to the prodigal son coming to himself and coming home, and to Jesus announcing that the Jubilee had arrived." },
      { "title": "Under-rowers in the engine room", "text": "The word Paul uses for servants of Christ can describe the lowest-ranked rowers on the bottom bench of an ancient galley, the invisible part of the operation. Apostles are not titled celebrities but the engine room crew." },
      { "title": "The artesian well", "text": "An artesian well is drilled into rock fed from higher ground, so water rises by its own pressure. The book uses it for the Spirit gushing up within us, linked to Jesus at the Feast of Tabernacles crying out that rivers of living water would flow from within (John 7:37-39)." },
      { "title": "The victory parade", "text": "Ancient triumphs were full of perfume and flower garlands. Paul pictures God leading us in his triumph and spreading the fragrance of Christ everywhere (2 Corinthians 2:14-16): to life it smells like life, and to the old system of works it smells like defeat." },
      { "title": "Gideon in the winepress", "text": "Gideon was secretly threshing wheat in a winepress, surviving in fear, when the angel called him a mighty man of valour (Judges 6:11-16). God saw far more in him than he saw in himself." },
      { "title": "The almond tree", "text": "God showed Jeremiah an almond branch, the tree that wakes first while others still sleep through winter, and said he was watching over his word to perform it (Jeremiah 1:11-12). In Hebrew 'almond' and 'watching' sound alike." },
      { "title": "A city through one woman", "text": "Jesus never underestimated anyone, whatever their reputation. Through one Samaritan woman the town of Sychar came to hear him, and they told her they now believed for themselves that he is the Saviour of the world (John 4:39-42)." },
      { "title": "Ten cities in one man", "text": "The man freed from a legion of demons begged to go with Jesus, but was sent home to tell what the Lord had done (Mark 5:18-20). He told it across the Decapolis, which means 'ten cities', and the book says Jesus saw ten cities in one man's story." },
      { "title": "All Asia from one school", "text": "In Ephesus Paul taught daily in the lecture hall of Tyrannus for two years, so that everyone living in the province of Asia, Jews and Greeks, heard the word (Acts 19:8-10). Focused teaching in one place reached a whole region." },
      { "title": "The ripe harvest", "text": "The disciples said harvest was four months away; Jesus told them to lift their eyes, because the fields were already ripe (John 4:35-38). The book notes a crop is ripe when the seed in the ear matches the seed sown, and sees people as already ready to discover they are included." },
      { "title": "Esther on the third day", "text": "On the third day Esther put on her royal robes, stood before the king, found favour and touched his golden sceptre (Esther 5:1-2). The book places this beside Jonah's three days and Hosea 6:2 as third-day pictures of humanity raised and welcomed with Christ." },
      { "title": "A continent in one man", "text": "Philip left a busy revival in Samaria for a desert road to meet one Ethiopian official. The book says God saw a whole continent in that one man." },
      { "title": "Ezekiel's dry bones", "text": "God showed Ezekiel bones with no sign of life and asked if they could live. The book pairs this with Sarah's barren womb and Adam created on the sixth day when all the work was done: God often acts when human effort has nothing left to offer." },
      { "title": "The soccer player who never scores", "text": "A footballer can do magic with the ball, but it means little if he never takes the gap and scores. Paul ran to win: his aim was not display but people actually grasping the gospel (1 Corinthians 9:24-27)." },
      { "title": "Clay jars and pearls", "text": "Paul says we hold treasure in clay jars. The book links the Greek word for clay to the word behind 'oyster' and pictures a precious pearl inside a plain shell; this word link is loose, but the point stands: the container's looks can never compete with what it holds." }
    ],
    "words": [
      { "word": "proorizo", "meaning": "To mark out beforehand, predestine. The book pictures an architect drawing a detailed plan, a horizon set in advance." },
      { "word": "airo", "meaning": "To lift up, carry, take away (John 1:29). Pictured as raising an anchor." },
      { "word": "kosmos", "meaning": "World, ordered system. Here used for the whole human family and its social structures." },
      { "word": "paraptoma", "meaning": "Trespass, false step. The book pictures falling from a height, losing altitude." },
      { "word": "metanoia", "meaning": "Change of mind, repentance; literally a 'with-mind' or after-thought. The book stresses a radical mind-shift rather than penance." },
      { "word": "homologeo", "meaning": "To confess, literally to say the same thing. Confession is agreeing with what God says." },
      { "word": "anagennao", "meaning": "To beget again, give new birth (1 Peter 1:3). Linked by the book to being born from above." },
      { "word": "chrio / mashach", "meaning": "To anoint (Greek and Hebrew). The book links both to drawing a hand over something, measuring it." },
      { "word": "aphesis", "meaning": "Release, forgiveness. The same word the Greek Old Testament uses for the Jubilee release." },
      { "word": "eudokia", "meaning": "Goodwill, good pleasure, delight (Luke 2:14). The book reads it as God's delight in humanity." },
      { "word": "huperetes", "meaning": "Servant, attendant; popularly explained as an under-rower in a galley." },
      { "word": "metadidomi", "meaning": "To share, impart (1 Thessalonians 2:8). A giving where the giver is wrapped up in the gift." },
      { "word": "kabod", "meaning": "Hebrew for glory, with the root sense of weight or heaviness. Paul's 'weight of glory' (2 Corinthians 4:17) echoes it." },
      { "word": "kapeleuo", "meaning": "To peddle, sell at retail (2 Corinthians 2:17). The book calls it putting a price tag on the gospel." },
      { "word": "eklegomai", "meaning": "To choose, elect. The book stresses its parts, 'out of' and 'word or logic', as God's blueprint opinion." }
    ],
    "questions": [
      { "q": "Does 'one died for all, therefore all died' mean everyone is automatically saved?", "a": "The book reads it as God's settled verdict over all humanity, which people are invited to wake up to. Many Christians agree Christ died for all but hold that salvation is received personally through faith, and Reformed readers often limit 'all' to the elect. The book itself still urges announcing the news so people can discover it." },
      { "q": "Was the word 'repent' really invented to make money?", "a": "No, that part goes beyond the evidence. The Latin Vulgate's 'do penance' did shape medieval practice, and Erasmus and Luther pushed back by stressing that <em>metanoia</em> means a change of mind. But 'repent' comes into English through Old French, and in the New Testament <em>metanoia</em> includes turning away from sin, not only a new perspective." },
      { "q": "Why does the chapter spend so long on Paul's ministry?", "a": "Because it sees Paul as the model of how inclusion shapes ministry. Persuaded that everyone is included in Christ, he could become all things to all people, refuse payment, endure suffering and treat no one as an outsider. His aim was not to impress people with himself but to awaken them to Christ in them." },
      { "q": "If the harvest is already ripe, why preach at all?", "a": "The book says people need to hear the news in order to see who they already are: how can they believe what they have not heard (Romans 10:14)? Preaching does not make the harvest ripe; it gathers what is ready. That is why the chapter calls every believer to be a herald." },
      { "q": "What should readers make of the numerology and letter readings around Abraham?", "a": "The added letter <em>he</em> in Abraham's name is real, and Genesis 17:5 does explain the name as 'father of a multitude'. Links to the number five as grace, or to Arabic words for rain, are the book's devotional speculations rather than established scholarship. They are best read as meditations, not proofs." }
    ],
    "scriptures": ["Romans 16:5", "2 Corinthians 5:14-16", "1 Corinthians 2:1-8", "John 1:29", "Romans 4:16-25", "Acts 17:27-31", "1 Peter 1:3-12", "Colossians 1:25-29", "Galatians 3:8-16", "Romans 1:14-17", "Romans 10:2-18", "Hosea 6:1-3", "Ephesians 2:5-6", "Luke 4:18-21", "Luke 2:10-14", "2 Corinthians 4:1-18", "2 Corinthians 11:23-30", "1 Corinthians 9:14-27", "Titus 3:2-6", "John 4:35-42", "Acts 19:8-10"],
    "reflect": "Who is the one person through whom God might reach a whole circle of people near you, and could that person be you?"
  },

  {
    "id": "ch14-nothing-wrong-with-you",
    "n": 14,
    "title": "There is nothing wrong with you!",
    "big": "Because your design and your salvation are both whole, what needs fixing is not you but the way you think about yourself.",
    "summary": "This short chapter pushes back against the popular advice to embrace your flaws and imperfections, saying it may be sincere psychology but it is not the good news. The gospel says you are complete and lacking nothing, because your design defines you, not the size of your struggles. You cannot measure your worth with the wrong instrument, the ruler of performance; the true measure is God's love and belief in you as seen in Christ. James 1 shows that joy and steady patience flow from knowing who you are, and that the only lack is wisdom, which God gives freely. The chapter recalls a moment of insight that there is nothing wrong with the human race's design or salvation, only with its thinking, and it raises the questions the next chapters take up.",
    "points": [
      { "head": "Not the gospel of embracing your flaws", "text": "We are often told to embrace our doubts, fears and imperfections. The book grants that this may be popular philosophy and sincere psychology, but says it is not the good news. The good news is that you are complete and lacking nothing." },
      { "head": "Your design defines you", "text": "Who you are is set by your design, not by the size of your challenges and contradictions. You cannot measure temperature with a ruler, and you cannot measure yourself by the law of willpower and performance. The right measure is the law of love and faith: God's love for you and belief in you, mirrored in Christ." },
      { "head": "Comparison misses the point", "text": "2 Corinthians 10:12 says those who measure themselves by one another and compare themselves with one another are without understanding. The book uses this to warn against building identity on competition. Song of Songs 4:7 says: you are all beautiful, my love; there is no spot in you." },
      { "head": "Faces that shine", "text": "The book cites the late Rob Lacey's lively street-language paraphrases of 2 Corinthians 3:18 and Psalm 34:5. Both picture faces with the masks off, glowing because they have looked at God. Psalm 34:5 in the WEB says: <em>They looked to him, and were radiant.</em>" },
      { "head": "God does not need 'Jesus glasses'", "text": "Some teach that God can only bear to look at us through Jesus, as if through special glasses. The book rejects this: God sees you, really you, and loves you. Jesus came to introduce us to ourselves again, so that we may know as we have always been known (1 Corinthians 13:12)." },
      { "head": "Joy that knows who you are", "text": "James 1:2-3 says to count it all joy when trials come. The book says trials try to pull you into their energy field, but joy in who you know you are leads you out every time; the word <em>hegeomai</em> (count, consider) is linked to leading with authority. Joy is not faked; it is the fruit of what faith knows to be true, and tested faith becomes steady persuasion." },
      { "head": "Patience proves you whole", "text": "James 1:4 says let endurance do its full work so that you may be perfect and complete, lacking nothing. The book pictures a mother hen patiently brooding over her eggs: steady endurance gives a stable environment in which your wholeness shows itself. The point is not that patience makes you whole, but that it reveals how whole you already are." },
      { "head": "Wisdom drawn straight from the source", "text": "James 1:5 says if anyone lacks wisdom, let them ask God, who gives generously. The book says wisdom is the only thing you could lack, and you can draw it directly from God rather than filtered through other opinions. It reads <em>haplos</em>, generously or simply, as 'woven together', the same idea as the 'single' eye full of light (Matthew 6:22), and links it to the Hebrew <em>qavah</em> in Isaiah 40:31, which has the root sense of a twisted cord: those who wait on, or entwine with, the Lord rise on eagles' wings. We are wired to entwine with God's thoughts." },
      { "head": "Enter God's rest", "text": "God's rest celebrates your completeness: he looked at what he made and called it very good. Zephaniah 3:17 pictures God resting in his love and rejoicing over you with singing. Hebrews 4:10-11 invites us to cease from our own works and enter his rest, which the book puts as: stop trying your best and enter his rest. Then live the adventure of your life from that place of strength (Colossians 3:1-4), telling your soul, as Psalm 116:7 does, to return to its rest because the Lord has dealt bountifully with you." },
      { "head": "A 2 a.m. insight in Budapest", "text": "The author recalls waking at two in the morning while ministering in Budapest in October 2010 with a startling thought. There is nothing wrong with the human race, because there is nothing wrong with its design and nothing wrong with its salvation; there is only something badly wrong with its thinking. Jesus is the proof that God has done enough to rescue his image and likeness in us and restore us to blameless innocence." },
      { "head": "Then why is the world such a mess?", "text": "If the cross was a success, why is the world still in such trouble? The book says it will deal with this in the following chapters: on wrong thinking, on the so-called sinful nature, and on why Jesus had to die. It also raises its own hardest question: if the problem was only in our minds and God never saw us differently, why could Jesus not simply come, reveal himself and remind us of our design, without dying?" }
    ],
    "illustrations": [
      { "title": "Measuring temperature with a ruler", "text": "A ruler is a fine tool, but useless for measuring heat. In the same way the ruler of performance can never measure a person's worth; only God's love and faith can." },
      { "title": "Jesus glasses", "text": "The book mocks the idea that God wears special 'Jesus glasses' so he can bear to look at us. Instead God looks straight at you and loves what he sees." },
      { "title": "The brooding hen", "text": "A mother hen sits patiently on her eggs, keeping them warm and steady until they hatch. Patience in trials is pictured the same way: a steady environment in which our wholeness comes to light." },
      { "title": "Waking in Budapest", "text": "In October 2010, during a ministry trip, the author woke at 2 a.m. with the thought that the human race's design and salvation are fine and only its thinking is wrong. That moment frames this chapter and the next." }
    ],
    "words": [
      { "word": "hegeomai", "meaning": "To lead, or to consider and count (James 1:2). The book plays on both senses: joy leads you out of trials in triumph." },
      { "word": "teleios", "meaning": "Complete, mature, perfect (James 1:4). Wholeness without shortfall." },
      { "word": "haplos", "meaning": "Generously, simply, with single-minded openness (James 1:5). The book explains it as woven together." },
      { "word": "qavah", "meaning": "Hebrew, to wait for or hope in (Isaiah 40:31); its root is linked to twisting or binding a cord. The book reads it as entwining with God's thoughts." }
    ],
    "questions": [
      { "q": "Isn't it more honest to admit our flaws than to say nothing is wrong with us?", "a": "The book is not denying that people behave badly or struggle. It is saying our struggles do not define us: our design and our salvation in Christ do. Many Christians would add that Scripture also calls us to confess sin honestly (1 John 1:9), and the next chapter reframes confession as agreeing with what God says about us." },
      { "q": "If there is nothing wrong with us, why did Jesus need to die?", "a": "The book raises this question itself and promises an answer in a later chapter. For now it insists the cross was a success and the remaining problem is our thinking. Traditional theology would say the cross dealt with real guilt and a real breach, not only a misunderstanding, which is why this point is debated." },
      { "q": "What does 'quit trying your best' mean in practice?", "a": "It means stop trying to earn identity or acceptance by effort, and start from rest in what God has already declared. The book does not counsel laziness: it says live the adventure from that place of strength. Action flows from rest rather than toward it." }
    ],
    "scriptures": ["2 Corinthians 10:12", "Song of Songs 4:7", "2 Corinthians 3:18", "Psalm 34:5", "1 Corinthians 13:12", "James 1:2-5", "Matthew 6:22", "Isaiah 40:31", "Zephaniah 3:17", "Hebrews 4:10-11", "Colossians 3:1-4", "Psalm 116:7"],
    "reflect": "What ruler have you been using to measure your worth, and what would it mean to measure yourself by God's love instead?"
  },

  {
    "id": "ch15-thinking-wrong",
    "n": 15,
    "title": "We were thinking wrong!",
    "big": "Like Israel in the desert, humanity was rescued but kept believing a lie about itself, so the gospel's appeal is a change of mind: to see ourselves in Christ as in a mirror.",
    "summary": "The chapter starts with Israel, freed from Egypt yet dying in the wilderness because they believed they were grasshoppers. It reads Isaiah 55 not as a statement that God's thoughts are beyond us, but as a promise that his thoughts will come down like rain and take on flesh, which they did in Jesus. Paul's veil passage in 2 Corinthians 3 explains why people still do not see: in Adam all were condemned before the law showed it, and in Christ all are declared innocent before the gospel shows it. When anyone turns to the Lord the veil is lifted and, seeing Christ as in a mirror, we discover our own true face; the change is not slow self-improvement but waking up to a completeness already ours. The chapter covers the single eye, confessing as saying what God says, renewal in the spirit of the mind, idolatry as worshipping a substitute image, the medieval Irish teacher Eriugena on Christ as our memory, and a Moroccan legend about Eve and a reflection.",
    "points": [
      { "head": "Rescued but still thinking like slaves", "text": "Israel was dramatically freed from Pharaoh but kept believing a lie about themselves and died, neutralised, in the desert (Numbers 13:33). Our personal and global challenges can likewise make us feel tiny. The book says the problem is not our rescue but our thinking." },
      { "head": "Isaiah 55 is a promise, not a wall", "text": "Isaiah 55:8-9 says God's thoughts are not our thoughts. The book says people often stop reading there and conclude God is unknowable, but verses 10-11 say his word comes down like rain and snow, soaks the earth and does not return empty. Jesus is that word made flesh, God's thoughts perfectly packaged in a human life, and the distance is cancelled. As head of the human race he fulfilled the word on our behalf: not an example for us, but of us." },
      { "head": "Condemned unaware, acquitted unaware", "text": "In 2 Corinthians 3:13-15 Moses veiled his face, and to this day a veil lies over hearts when the old covenant is read. The tablets of stone only confirmed humanity's death and the futility of trying to match our design by works. The book's summary: in Adam all stood condemned but did not realise it until the law revealed it; in Christ all are declared innocent but do not know it until the gospel reveals it. The old system is rendered useless (<em>katargeo</em>), freeing us from trying to improve what God has already perfected." },
      { "head": "When anyone turns, the veil is gone", "text": "2 Corinthians 3:16 says when one turns to the Lord the veil is taken away. <em>Epistrepho</em> means to return to where we wandered from: we went astray like sheep and have now returned to the Shepherd and Guardian of our souls (1 Peter 2:25). Hebrews 8:10 promises God will write his laws on minds and hearts, so it is no longer one-sided. James 1:25 calls this the perfect law of liberty, the image of God revealed in Christ and now seen in us." },
      { "head": "Seeing ourselves in the mirror", "text": "2 Corinthians 3:17-18 says where the Spirit of the Lord is there is freedom, and we all, with unveiled faces, behold the Lord's glory as in a mirror and are transformed into the same image. <em>Katoptrizomai</em> means to look into a mirror, and <em>metamorphoo</em>, to be transformed, is set by the book against its reading of sin as being 'without form'. Gazing at God's likeness in Christ, we realise we are looking at ourselves. The two glories in the chapter are the fading glory of the flesh, represented by Moses, and the unfading glory of God's image in the face of Christ." },
      { "head": "Not a lifetime of slow change", "text": "'From glory to glory' is often taken to mean gradual change over a lifetime, but the book calls that the kind of thinking that kept Israel wandering forty years. We cannot become more than we already are in Christ: we do not grow more complete, we grow in awareness of our completeness (Colossians 3:10). It notes that Paul uses <em>apo</em> (away from) here, whereas Romans 1:17 uses <em>ek</em> (out of): we move away from the glory of our own achievements and failures to the glory of our original design. Many readers would still see a real, ongoing process in this verse, since the verb is in the present tense." },
      { "head": "Dead to the old self, alive to the real one", "text": "The 'ugly duckling' mindset died with Christ; God did not rescue a compromised copy of you but the original, blueprint you. Romans 6:11 calls us to count the do-it-yourself, law-of-works self as dead and the true self as raised with Christ. Galatians 2:20 says I have been crucified with Christ, and the book notes the perfect tense: a completed act with lasting effect, so I am dead to the self I was trying to be and alive to the real me, Christ in me. Romans 7:6 adds that we now serve in the newness of the Spirit, and the moment rules replace spontaneity, the romance is lost." },
      { "head": "The god of this age", "text": "2 Corinthians 4:4 says the god of this age has blinded unbelieving minds so they cannot see the light of the gospel of Christ's glory. The book identifies this 'god' with the religious and governing systems of self-improvement and survival that exploit ignorance of our true origin. The unbelief that stalled Israel was the lie they believed about themselves. Most commentators take 'the god of this age' to mean Satan, so this reading is the book's own emphasis." },
      { "head": "The single eye and the hidden basket", "text": "Luke 11:34-36 says when your eye is single (<em>haplous</em>) your whole body is full of light, but when it is evil (<em>poneros</em>) it is full of darkness. The book reads <em>haplous</em> as woven together with God's view of you, and <em>poneros</em> as the weary system of hardship and toil. It notes the lamp is not hidden in a cellar (<em>krupte</em>) or under a measuring basket (<em>modios</em>, a grain measure of a little under nine litres), which it reads as secret agendas and transactional living. Let God's light flood every hidden corner until your whole life shines." },
      { "head": "Confession: saying what God says", "text": "1 John 1:8-9 says if we claim to have no sin we deceive ourselves, but if we confess our sins he is faithful to forgive and cleanse. The book reads the first as claiming innocence by our own performance, and stresses that <em>homologeo</em>, confess, means to say the same thing: when we say what God says about our sins, we discover what he believes about our oneness and innocence. John 1:3-5 and Isaiah 9:2 add that darkness, meaning ignorance of our redeemed identity, could not overcome the light." },
      { "head": "Truth given a voice by love", "text": "Ephesians 4:14-16 says an ignorant life is tossed like an infant on a ship in a storm, at the mercy of dice and clever tricks. Speaking the truth in love creates the atmosphere where growth is spontaneous. The book explains <em>agape</em> as from words for leading and resting, seeing the value God sees in every person (a popular etymology, not a standard one), and <em>epichoregeo</em>, supply, as like a conductor bringing a choir into harmony. The church is pictured, with a nod to Rob Lacey, as a fully coordinated body, not a dismembered one." },
      { "head": "Renewed in the spirit of your mind", "text": "Ephesians 4:17-24 describes minds darkened by ignorance and calloused hearts, then says: Christ is the total contrast. Strip off the old identity like an actor's costume that no longer fits the role, and be renewed in the spirit of your mind. The book notes Paul does not say 'renew your minds' as a task but describes a renewal awakened deep within by truth, not mere head knowledge; it is not that information must drop from head to heart but the other way round. The human spirit was never contaminated, like a watermark in a banknote; the coin never lost its image. Our minds were darkened, so Jesus entered our worst darkness, died our death, and co-raised us so we can see clearly again." },
      { "head": "Whatever he is as Son, we are", "text": "1 John 5:19 says the whole world lies in the power of the evil one, which the book reads as lying stretched out (<em>keimai</em>) in the blindfold of a lost identity. Verse 20 says the Son of God has come (<em>heko</em>, has arrived and is present) and has given us understanding (<em>dianoia</em>) to know him who is true, and we are in him who is true. With 1 John 4:17 (as he is, so are we in this world) and John 14:20, the book concludes that whatever Jesus is as Son, we are." },
      { "head": "Idols are substitute images", "text": "1 John 5:21 ends: keep yourselves from idols. <em>Eidolon</em> means an image or likeness, so the book says idolatry is any substitute image that competes with our true likeness. Isaiah 40:18-21 mocks the craftsman's idol and asks: have you not known from the beginning? Paul tells the Athenians that since we are God's offspring we should not imagine God as gold, silver or stone (Acts 17:28-30). The book calls idolatry the crux of religion and compares an idol to a casino slot machine that stays hungry and bankrupts you." },
      { "head": "Change your mind about you", "text": "The appeal of the good news is to straighten out our thinking, which the book says is what <em>metanoia</em> means: to discover God's thoughts about you. It repeats its claim that 'repent' smuggled in the Latin idea of penance (see the note on chapter 13: the change-of-mind sense is sound, the money motive is not documented). Jeremiah 29:11 says God's thoughts toward us are of peace and not of evil, to give a future and a hope. Jesus is God's mind made up about humanity." },
      { "head": "Christ our memory, and the pool of reflection", "text": "The book draws on a modern writer on Celtic spirituality describing the ninth-century Irish teacher John Scotus Eriugena, who taught that Christ is our memory: we suffer from the soul's forgetfulness, and Christ comes to show us both God's face and our own. In that tradition nature and grace flow together from God, nature as the gift of being and grace as the gift of well-being, and grace opposes not what is deepest in us but what is false. It then retells a Moroccan legend in which Eve, told a lovelier woman was hiding in a cave, saw her own reflection in a pool, mistook it for a rival and ate the fruit; whoever is not deceived by the reflection stays in paradise. The chapter ends with Psalm 22, where the ends of the earth remember and turn to the Lord, and Psalm 23, where the soul is restored and walks through the valley of the shadow of death without fear." }
    ],
    "illustrations": [
      { "title": "Grasshoppers in the desert", "text": "Israel left Egypt free but saw themselves as grasshoppers next to giants. A whole generation died in the wilderness, not because their deliverance failed, but because of what they believed about themselves." },
      { "title": "Rain and snow", "text": "Rain and snow fall from the sky, soak the ground and make it sprout before they go back up. God says his word works the same way (Isaiah 55:10-11), and the book sees Jesus as that word fully landed in human flesh." },
      { "title": "The ugly duckling's illusion", "text": "How long does it take a swan to realise it is a swan? The ugly duckling was only ever an illusion, and the mindset that believed it died with Christ." },
      { "title": "The actor's cloak", "text": "An actor wears a costume for a particular role. Once the play is over, keeping it on makes no sense. The old identity is pictured as a worn-out costume to strip off." },
      { "title": "A watermark in a banknote", "text": "A watermark is built into the paper and cannot be scrubbed off by dirt or wear. The book uses it to say the human spirit was never contaminated; it was the mind that was darkened." },
      { "title": "The hidden basket", "text": "A lamp hidden in a cellar or under a grain-measuring basket gives no light. The book reads the basket as transactional, measure-for-measure living that keeps our light hidden." },
      { "title": "The casino slot machine", "text": "An idol is compared to a slot machine: always hungry, never satisfied, wired to take your money until you are broke. False images of God and of ourselves work the same way." },
      { "title": "Eve and the pool", "text": "In a Moroccan legend, the deceiver told Eve that a more beautiful woman was hiding in a cave and offered fruit to make her lovelier still. Looking into a pool in the cave she saw her own reflection, believed it was a rival, and ate. The legend ends: whoever is not fooled by the reflection stays in paradise. We could not verify the legend's source, so treat it as a story the book tells." }
    ],
    "words": [
      { "word": "katargeo", "meaning": "To render inactive, abolish (2 Corinthians 3:14). The old system of works is made useless." },
      { "word": "epistrepho", "meaning": "To turn, return (2 Corinthians 3:16). Coming back to where we wandered from." },
      { "word": "anakalupto", "meaning": "To unveil, uncover again (2 Corinthians 3:14,18). The unveiled face." },
      { "word": "katoptrizomai", "meaning": "To look into a mirror, to see as in a mirror (2 Corinthians 3:18)." },
      { "word": "metamorphoo", "meaning": "To transform, change form (2 Corinthians 3:18; Romans 12:2). Image and likeness waking up within us." },
      { "word": "haplous", "meaning": "Single, simple, clear (Luke 11:34). The book reads it as woven together with God's view." },
      { "word": "poneros", "meaning": "Evil, bad. The book links it to toil and hardship, the weary system of performance." },
      { "word": "modios", "meaning": "A grain-measuring basket, translated 'bushel' (Luke 11:33)." },
      { "word": "keimai", "meaning": "To lie, be laid out (1 John 5:19). The world lying in the evil one." },
      { "word": "dianoia", "meaning": "Understanding, the mind as a means of knowing (1 John 5:20)." },
      { "word": "eidolon", "meaning": "An image or likeness, an idol (1 John 5:21). A substitute image." },
      { "word": "epichoregeo", "meaning": "To supply, furnish (Ephesians 4:16; from the word for a chorus leader). Pictured as a conductor bringing harmony." }
    ],
    "questions": [
      { "q": "Does 'from glory to glory' mean change is gradual?", "a": "The book says no: we do not become more complete, we only grow in awareness of a completeness already ours. Many interpreters point to the present tense of 'being transformed' and to texts like Philippians 3:12-14 to say there is real, ongoing growth. Both sides agree that change comes from beholding Christ, not from self-effort." },
      { "q": "Is the 'god of this age' Satan or religion?", "a": "The book takes it as the religious and governing systems of works that keep people blind to their true identity. Most commentators read it as Satan, as in John 12:31 and Ephesians 2:2. The two readings overlap: both see something that blinds people to Christ's glory." },
      { "q": "If confession means 'saying what God says', do we still name our sins?", "a": "The book stresses agreeing with God's verdict of forgiveness and innocence. That does not have to rule out honest naming of wrongs; agreeing with God includes calling sin what it is and then believing what he says about our cleansing. Traditional readings of 1 John 1:9 emphasise the honest admission of specific sins." },
      { "q": "Why bring in Eriugena and a Moroccan legend?", "a": "Both illustrate the chapter's core idea: we have forgotten our true face, and salvation includes remembering it. Eriugena is a real ninth-century Irish theologian whose bold thought was later viewed with suspicion by church authorities. The legend is a story about mistaking our own reflection for a rival, which the book reads as the root of the fall." }
    ],
    "scriptures": ["Numbers 13:33", "Isaiah 55:8-11", "2 Corinthians 3:13-18", "1 Peter 2:25", "Hebrews 8:10", "James 1:25", "Romans 6:11", "Galatians 2:20", "Romans 7:6", "2 Corinthians 4:4", "Luke 11:33-36", "1 John 1:7-9", "Ephesians 4:14-24", "1 John 5:19-21", "Isaiah 40:18-21", "Acts 17:28-30", "Jeremiah 29:11", "Psalm 22:27", "Psalm 23"],
    "reflect": "When you look into the mirror of Christ, what do you see about yourself that you have been too afraid to believe?"
  }
];
